Nothing has been revealed and no trades have happened, so the public fair value is the prior mean of the sum, 3 × 3.5 = 10.5. The distribution is symmetric around 10.5, so I'll quote symmetrically.

For the ask side, with distance d = ask − 10.5, the expected profit per quote is roughly 0.65·P(noise valuation > ask)·d plus 0.35·E[(ask − S)·1{S > ask}] from informed buyers. Evaluating this for asks from 10.5 to 15.5 gives about −0.42 at 10.5, +0.26 at 12.5, +0.40 at 13.5, +0.43 at 14.5, and +0.40 at 15.5. The ask of 14.5 is the best of these, and the bid mirrors it at 6.5. These are the same quotes as the first stage-1 quote.

{"bid": 6.5, "ask": 14.5}

Public fair value is 4 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 11, since die 1 is revealed as 4 and dice 2 and 3 are unknown. The settlement value is 4 + (die 2 + die 3), which ranges from 6 to 16.

Against a symmetric spread around 11, I balanced the noise-trader edge (0.65 × (ask − 11) × P(noise valuation > ask)) against the informed-trader loss (0.35 × E[(V − ask)⁺]). Evaluating candidate half-spreads, the expected profit peaks near a half-spread of 3.8 and is flat between about 3.7 and 3.9. That gives bid 7.2 and ask 14.8.

{"bid": 7.2, "ask": 14.8}

Die 1 is 5 and the other two dice average 7 each, so the prior fair value is 12. The trade history pulls this down: nobody has bought at asks around 14.4 to 14.5, and a seller hit our 9.0 bid, which suggests the true sum is on the low side. Updating on those trades gives a posterior mean of about 11.0, which I use as the public fair value.

Using 11.0 and the 35% informed share, the expected profit per side is roughly flat across a wide band, with the best bid near 7.5 to 8 and the best ask near 14 to 14.5. I'll quote a bid of 8.0 and an ask of 14.2. The ask is a little tighter than the optimum, which helps reduce my +2 long position at no cost in expected value.

{"bid": 8.0, "ask": 14.2}

Stage 1 has no revealed information, so the public fair value is 10.5 by symmetry. The first quote drew no trade, which slightly favours middle sums, but the posterior mean stays at 10.5.

I balanced noise-trader edge against informed-trader adverse selection. Half-spreads of about 2.5 to 3 give similar expected profit, so I'm quoting a 5.0 spread centred on 10.5. The bid rounds down and the ask rounds up, which keeps both on the 0.1 grid.

{"bid": 8.0, "ask": 13.0}

The public fair value isn't 8. Die 1 = 1 gives a prior mean of 8, but the rules make the fair value condition on the trading history too. Four quotes with no trade mean the informed trader didn't hit any of them, so the settlement is more likely to lie inside those spreads, especially 9 to 11. Updating on that gives a fair value of about 9.4.

Given that centre, I computed expected profit for candidate quotes. The noise-trader edge is strongest on the ask side and the informed-seller risk sits on the low side, so the best quote is a bid near 7 and an ask of 12. Values between 6.8 and 7.0 are roughly equal.

{"bid": 7.0, "ask": 12.0}

Die 1 is 5, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12. Stage 1 quotes were centred near 10.5, so I'm moving the centre up. The remaining uncertainty is smaller (two unrevealed dice, sd about 2.4), so I'm tightening the half-spread to about 3. I'm also skewing the quotes down slightly because I'm long +1, which makes me prefer selling to buying.

Bid 8.8 and ask 14.7 are centred at 11.75 with a half-spread of about 2.95.

{"bid": 8.8, "ask": 14.7}

Die 1 is 5, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12.0. Noise traders value the contract around 12 with sd 4.4. Informed traders know the exact sum, which spans 7 to 17 around 12.

Quoting at 12 ± h, the expected profit on each side is the noise-trader edge, 0.8·h·P(noise trader crosses), minus the informed-trader loss, 0.2·E[(V − quote)⁺]. Maximising each side gives h ≈ 3.5. Since I'm risk-neutral and far from the ±3 limits, my long position of +1 doesn't change this, so I'm not skewing the quote.

{"bid": 8.5, "ask": 15.5}

The fair value starts at 10.5, the mean of three dice. Noise traders buy or sell roughly at fair value plus a N(0, 3) valuation, so a wide spread earns on them. Informed traders hit whichever side is wrong, and their expected loss shrinks as the spread widens.

Modelling it roughly, the ask at 13.5 (3 above fair value) maximises expected profit on that side. The per-side expected profit is about 0.22 at 13.5, slightly below 13.6 and clearly above 14.0. A symmetric bid at 21 − 13.5 = 7.5 is the mirror image. A tight spread near 10.5 loses money overall, because informed flow costs more than noise flow pays.

{"bid": 7.5, "ask": 13.5}

Public fair value is the expected sum of three fair d6s: 3 × 3.5 = 10.5. No dice are revealed yet, so I quote symmetrically around 10.5.

Each side's expected profit is roughly 0.8 × d × P(noise trader trades) minus 0.2 × E[informed trader's edge], where d is the half-spread. Evaluating over the dice-sum distribution, the best half-spread is about 3.5, which gives an expected profit of about 0.56 per side. That corresponds to a bid of 7.0 and an ask of 14.0.

{"bid": 7.0, "ask": 14.0}

Nothing has been revealed and no trade has happened, so the public fair value is still 10.5 (the mean of three fair dice). The bid and ask are symmetric around that value. The previous quote of 7/14 was centred on 10.5, and no trade means no new information, so I'll keep it.

A rough check of the ask side supports this. Expected profit from the ask is the noise-trader flow times the spread over fair value, minus the informed-trader loss. At an ask of 14 that comes to about +0.57 per quote, close to the best ask I checked (14.5 gave about +0.56). The bid side mirrors it, since the dice sum and the noise distribution are both symmetric about 10.5.

{"bid": 7, "ask": 14}

Die 2 and die 3 are still unknown, so the public fair value is 3 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 10. The true value S is symmetric around 10, with range 5 to 15.

I chose the ask that balances noise-trader edge against adverse selection from informed buyers. Solving that first-order condition gives an ask of about 13.5, and by symmetry the bid is about 6.5. Both round cleanly to the 0.1 grid.

{"bid": 6.5, "ask": 13.5}

Fair value is 3 + 4 + 3.5 = 10.5, since die 3 is still unknown. Noise valuations are centred there with sd 4.4. Informed traders know the settlement value S = 7 + die 3, which is uniform on 8 to 13, so they never trade at an ask of 13 or above, or at a bid of 8 or below.

Per quote, my expected profit is roughly 0.8 × P(noise trade) × edge + 0.2 × (informed trade losses). Above 13 only noise traders trade, and the edge-times-probability term peaks where z = (A − 10.5)/4.4 ≈ 0.75, which is an ask near 13.8. By symmetry the bid is near 7.2. Both sit outside the informed trader's range, so there is no adverse-selection cost at these prices.

{"bid": 7.2, "ask": 13.8}

Fair value is 3 + 4 + 3.5 = 10.5, since die 3 is still unknown (mean 3.5). The informed trader knows the settlement value S = 7 + die 3, so S is in {8, ..., 13}. That means an ask at 13 or above and a bid at 8 or below can't be picked off by an informed buyer or seller.

Under that constraint, the expected profit from a noise trade is the distance from fair value. Its probability is 0.8 times the chance the noise valuation crosses my quote, which is N(10.5, 4.4) on the ask side and the mirror image on the bid side. Expected profit per side peaks near 0.6 at an ask of 13.7 and a bid of 7.3. Both prices are outside the informed range, so there is no adverse selection at these levels. Position doesn't change expected profit here, so I'm not adjusting for inventory.

{"bid": 7.3, "ask": 13.7}

Die 1 is 6, so the public fair value is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. Position is flat, so I quote symmetrically around 13.

Treating noise-trader valuations as Normal(13, 3) and informed traders as knowing the exact sum (8 to 18), the expected profit per side peaks at about 2.5 from fair value after accounting for adverse selection. That gives an ask near 15.5 and, by symmetry, a bid near 10.5.

{"bid": 10.5, "ask": 15.5}

Die 1 is 6, and dice 2 and 3 are unknown, so the public fair value is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. My position is 0, so there is no inventory reason to skew the quotes. I'll quote symmetrically around 13.

The half-spread trades off noise-trader fills, which earn roughly the half-spread each, against adverse selection from informed traders. Working through the sum distribution, the expected profit per side is maximised near a half-spread of 2.5, which gives a bid of 10.5 and an ask of 15.5. This matches my quote 4 and is slightly better than wider or narrower spreads.

{"bid": 10.5, "ask": 15.5}

Die 1 is 6, so the public fair value is 6 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 13. Position and cash don't change the expected settlement, so I'm quoting around 13 and not skewing for the -1 position.

Quotes that balance noise-trader edge against adverse selection from the 20% informed flow land at about ±2.6 from 13. The informed term comes from E[(S-A)^+] over the sum distribution, which is symmetric about 13. That gives an ask near 15.6 and, by symmetry, a bid near 10.4.

{"bid": 10.4, "ask": 15.6}

Die 1 is 5 and the other two dice are unrevealed, so the public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12. The sum is symmetric around 12, and the noise traders are centred on 12 with SD 3.

Each side's expected profit is the noise-trader spread capture minus the informed-trader adverse selection. Under these assumptions the ask around 14.5 maximises the ask-side profit, and by symmetry the bid around 9.5 maximises the bid side. Both quotes sit about 2.5 from fair value, and the position is flat.

{"bid": 9.5, "ask": 14.5}

Public fair value is 5 + 3.5 + 3.5 = 12. Settlement is 5 + (die 2 + die 3), so it is symmetric around 12 with standard deviation about 2.4.

Against the noise traders, a half-spread d gives expected profit 0.8 × P(noise valuation beyond the quote) × d. Against informed traders it costs 0.2 × E[(V − quote)⁺]. Quoting at fair value loses about 0.19 per side to informed flow. For ask offsets of d = 2, 2.5 and 3, the expected profit per side is about 0.35, 0.37 and 0.36, so d = 2.5 is near the optimum. Position is flat, so no inventory skew is needed.

{"bid": 9.5, "ask": 14.5}

Fair value is 14.5: die 1 + die 2 = 11 is known, and die 3 is uniform on 1 to 6 (mean 3.5). So the settlement is uniform on {12, ..., 17}.

Without inventory, the expected-profit-maximising quotes are symmetric around 14.5 at roughly bid 12 and ask 17, since the informed trader can only hit a quote when it is on the wrong side of the settlement value. I'm short 1, so I shade both sides up a little to make buying back more likely: bid 12.8, ask 17.2. The ask is above the top possible settlement of 17, so an informed buyer can never take it.

{"bid": 12.8, "ask": 17.2}

Public fair value is 0 (die 1, revealed) + 2.5 (die 2 mean) + 6.25 (die 3 mean) = 8.75. Die 3 is still hidden, so the informed counterparty knows more than we do.

Quoting against the 64 equally likely outcomes of S = d2 + d3 and the noise traders (mean 8.75, sd 5.8), the expected profit per quote is about +0.36 on the bid at 2 and about +0.35 on the ask at 15. Both sides are positive and near their optimum, and the current quotes are unchanged from quote 4.

{"bid": 2, "ask": 15}

Public fair value before any trade was 18.4 (the sum of the expected die values, 6.75 + 6.8 + 4.9). The sell at my 10.7 bid is evidence the sum is low, since informed sellers only hit a bid below the true value. Updating on that puts the posterior fair value near 13, and noise traders will price off that.

Adverse selection is heavy here because the informed side knows the sum exactly. Working each side separately with noise-trader valuations centred near 13 and an informed-value posterior, the expected profit per side peaks around a bid of about 6 and an ask of about 22.5. The bid and ask decisions don't interact, since only one counterparty arrives per quote. Being long 1 doesn't change the calculation, because the position is already marked at the same fair value.

{"bid": 6.0, "ask": 22.5}

Under the prior, the expected settlement is 6.75 + 6.8 + 4.875 ≈ 18.4. The two sells at our bids (10.7, then 6) are strong evidence that the value is low: informed sellers only hit a bid when the value is below it. Conditioning on that trading history puts the public fair value near 8, with a bimodal posterior (most mass near 4, about 20% chance of a high value near 20).

With that posterior, an ask much below about 14 loses money to informed buyers, and asks around 15 are roughly break-even to slightly positive. A bid around 2.5 earns about +0.5 in expectation, since informed sellers are rare at that level.

{"bid": 2.5, "ask": 15.0}

The fair value is 16.55: die 1 averages 6.75, unrevealed die 2 averages 6.8, and die 3 is known to be 3. The bid is pulled at +3, so I'm quoting only the ask.

Settlement is V = S1 + S2 + 3, with a standard deviation of about 5.9. Noise traders buy when 16.55 + ε > ask, with ε ~ N(0, 6.5²). Informed traders buy when V > ask. Maximising expected profit from a sale at ask A over this mix gives A = 24.0. There the informed-buyer loss is small because only V = 25 or 26 trade, and the noise-trader premium is large. Nearby asks (23.9, 24.1, 23) all do worse, and this matches the ask of 24 I posted at quote 5 under the same information.

{"bid": null, "ask": 24}

Fair value is the sum of the expected dice: 3.75 + 2.5 + 6.25 = 12.5. The sum's standard deviation is about 5.8, matching the noise traders' spread.

Because the informed trader knows the sum, I quoted wider than the noise spread would suggest. Against the exact sum distribution, the expected profit per side is roughly flat around these prices. The distribution is skewed (die 1 can be 10 and die 3 can reach 9), so the bid sits further from 12.5 than the ask. Bid 6.0 gives about 6.5 below fair value and ask 19.5 about 7 above.

{"bid": 6.0, "ask": 19.5}

Fair value is the expected sum: die 1 averages 7, die 2 averages 4.6 (0.8 + 0.2 + 1.2 + 2.4), and die 3 averages 8, so the public fair value is 19.6. The total variance is about 13.1 (standard deviation about 3.6), close to the noise-trader spread.

With a 35% chance of an informed counterparty, quoting near 19.6 loses money to adverse selection. A rough calculation of expected profit per side, treating the sum as normal with standard deviation 3.6, gives a best half-spread of about 3.5 to 4. That yields a net expected edge of roughly 0.26 per side. I'll quote a symmetric market around 19.6 with a half-spread of about 3.8.

{"bid": 15.8, "ask": 23.4}

Without any revealed dice, the fair value is the expected sum: 7 (die 1) + 4.6 (die 2) + 8 (die 3) = 19.6. The sum's standard deviation is about 3.6, which matches the noise-trader sd.

For a symmetric half-spread d, the noise-trader gain is roughly 0.65·d·Φ(−d/3.6), and the informed-trader loss is 0.35·E[(V−ask)⁺]. Maximising the net per side gives d ≈ 3.6, so I'll quote about 3.6 either side of 19.6. Rounding bids down and asks up gives 16.0 and 23.2.

{"bid": 16.0, "ask": 23.2}

Fair value before any reveals is E[die 1] + E[die 2] + E[die 3] = 7 + 4.6 + 8 = 19.6. The sum's standard deviation is about 3.6, close to the noise traders' 3.6.

Quoting around 19.6 means balancing noise-trader spread capture against informed-trader adverse selection. The informed trader's exposure is set by the true sum's tails, which are somewhat heavier than normal here because the loaded die 2 creates clusters near 17, 21 and 23. Working through the marginal profit of each side, the optimal ask is about 23.3 and the optimal bid about 15.9. Both sit close to the previous quotes, and I'm keeping the spread wide enough to cover the informed flow.

{"bid": 15.9, "ask": 23.3}

Fair value is 8 (die 1, revealed) + 4.6 (die 2, loaded: 0.8+0.2+1.2+2.4) + 8 (die 3, uniform over 7, 7, 8, 10) = 20.6. Die 2 is still hidden, so I can't use its reveal yet.

I computed the settlement distribution exactly (V from 17 to 26, mean 20.6) and maximised one-step expected profit against the mix of noise traders (N(20.6, 3.6), 65%) and informed traders (knowing V, 35%). The best ask is about 23.9 to 24.0, and rounding up gives 24.0. The best bid is 17.0, since moving above 17 would start attracting informed sellers (P(V=17) = 0.25). The quotes are close to the previous 17 / 24, which is consistent.

{"bid": 17.0, "ask": 24.0}

Given the revealed dice, the sum is 8 + 6 + D3 = 14 + D3, where D3 is 7, 7, 8 or 10. So the settlement value V is 21 (prob 1/2), 22 (1/4) or 24 (1/4), with public fair value E[V] = 22.

Informed traders only trade against quotes they can profit from, so I place the quotes outside the range where they would trade. The informed buyer only hits my ask if V > ask, and V never exceeds 24, so an ask above 24 is safe. Similarly, a bid below 21 is safe from the informed seller. Within those limits, noise-trader fill probability times edge is maximised at roughly 2.7 above and below the fair value of 22. That gives an ask of 24.7 and a bid of 19.3, with an expected edge of about 0.4 on each side.

{"bid": 19.3, "ask": 24.7}

The public fair value is 8 + 6 + E[die 3] = 14 + 8 = 22, where die 3 averages 8. Settlement is 21 with probability 0.5, 22 with probability 0.25, and 24 with probability 0.25.

Informed traders trade only when the settlement value is beyond my quote, so they adversely select my prices. Noise traders are uninformative, centred on 22 with standard deviation 3.6. Optimising expected profit on each side, the ask is where the noise-trader marginal gain balances the informed-trader loss, which gives about z = 0.75 above 22, or 24.7. The bid is symmetric and gives 19.3. Both match the quotes that have already been posted, and no trade has occurred, so the position is still 0.

{"bid": 19.3, "ask": 24.7}

Quote 1 got no trade, and nothing new has been revealed, so the fair value is still the prior mean. Die means are 5.75, 5 and 5.25, so the sum's expected value is 16.0.

With a 0.2 chance of an informed counterparty and noise valuations with standard deviation 6.6, the expected-profit-maximising half-spread solves Φ̄(u) = 0.8·u·φ(u), where u = d/6.6. That gives u ≈ 0.875, so d ≈ 5.8. The quote is 16 ± 5.8, which is 10.2 bid and 21.8 ask. It is the same as quote 1 but slightly tighter.

{"bid": 10.2, "ask": 21.8}

Fair value for quote 5: die 1 and die 2 are unrevealed, so their expected values are 5.75 and 5, and die 3 is known to be 7. That gives 17.75. The reveal of die 1 comes before stage 3, so it isn't yet known.

I quoted symmetrically around 17.75. Balancing noise-trader flow (sd 6.6) against informed flow (20%) gives a half-spread of about 5.5. The bid rounds down to 12.2 and the ask rounds up to 23.3.

{"bid": 12.2, "ask": 23.3}

Public fair value is 12 + E[die 2] = 12 + 5 = 17. Die 2 is unrevealed, so informed buyers' values are V ∈ {13, 14, 15, 16, 20, 24}.

Expected profit from the ask side, per quote, is 0.8·P(X>A)·(A−17) + (0.2/6)·Σ over V>A of (A−V), where X ~ N(17, 6.6). Maximising this gives an ask of about 22.6–22.7. The bid side, with the analogous formula, is maximised near 12.0. These match the spread the earlier quotes used. Inventory is +2, but at fair value holding the position has no expected cost, so I'm not skewing the quotes much.

{"bid": 12.0, "ask": 22.6}

Die 3 = 8 and die 1 = 8 are known, so the settlement is 16 + die 2, where die 2 is uniform on {0, 2, 4, 8, 8, 10}. That gives settlement values of {16, 18, 20, 24, 24, 26}, each with probability 1/6, and a public fair value of about 21.33.

Informed traders only trade when the settlement is outside our quotes: they sell to us if S < bid and buy from us if S > ask. Bid 16 and ask 26 sit exactly on the edges of the support, so an informed trader never trades against either side. Among nearby prices, these also come out closest to the best expected edge against noise traders (roughly 0.6 per quote on each side), and the ask at 26 is slightly better than the previous 26.9.

{"bid": 16, "ask": 26}

Fair value is 11 + 8 + E[die 3] = 11 + 8 + 8 = 27. The informed trader's settlement values are 26 (prob 1/2), 27 (1/4) and 29 (1/4).

Quoting outside the informed values avoids adverse selection, so the spread is set by the noise traders alone. Their valuations are N(27, 3.6). Each side's expected edge is (price − 27) × P(noise trader crosses), which is maximised where z·φ(z) = Φ̄(z), giving z ≈ 0.75 and an offset of about 2.7 from fair. The ask is 29.7, which is above every informed value. The bid is 24.2 (24.29 rounded down), which is below every informed value. Both sides are fine with a -2 position.

{"bid": 24.2, "ask": 29.7}

Public fair value is the sum of the expected faces: 5.75 + 5.00 + 5.25 = **16.0**.

The main risk is adverse selection from the 20% informed traders, who buy only when the true sum is above my ask and sell only when it is below my bid. Balancing the noise-trader spread income (sd 6.6) against that loss, the expected profit per quote peaks at a half-spread of about 6. The exact discrete distribution gives about 17% for the sum exceeding 22 and about 18% for it falling below 10, so the roughly symmetric quotes are reasonable. The spread should tighten after the reveals before stages 2 and 3.

{"bid": 10.0, "ask": 22.0}

Fair value is 16. The expected values of the three dice are 5.75, 5.00 and 5.25, which sum to 16.0. The variance of the sum is about 43.3 (sd about 6.6), similar to the noise traders' sd.

With no information revealed, I'll quote symmetrically around 16. A rough one-shot check, treating the sum as roughly normal, suggests a half-spread of about 1.2 is needed for the noise-trader edge to cover losses to informed traders. A bid of 14.8 and an ask of 17.2 meet that.

{"bid": 14.8, "ask": 17.2}

**Fair value:** Die 1 averages 5.75, die 2 averages 5.0, and the revealed die 3 is 6, so the public fair value is 16.75. Noise valuations are centred there with sd 6.6, which is wider than the true value's spread (about 5.0).

**Quote:** Noise traders only trade when their valuation is past my price, so trade probability falls as I move away from fair value. Informed traders always trade on the side that hurts me. Balancing these two effects, the expected profit per quote is highest around a bid of 11 and an ask of 22. Expected profit is linear in the settlement value, so my short position of -2 doesn't change the optimum, and I don't skew for it.

{"bid": 11.0, "ask": 22.0}

Fair value is E[die 1] + E[die 2] = 6 + 8 = 14. Die 2 is not revealed until stage 2, so the public fair value is 14 now.

Each side's quote trades off noise-trader flow against adverse selection from the 20% informed traders. Over the 24 equally likely sums, P(S > 18.4) ≈ 0.25 and P(S < 9.8) ≈ 0.21. The expected-profit-maximising ask is about 18.35, which rounds up to 18.4. The best bid is about 9.8, which is already a multiple of 0.1 and rounds down to itself. The spread is wide because the informed-flow penalty is largest near the centre of the distribution.

{"bid": 9.8, "ask": 18.4}

Public fair value is about 22.33 (die 1 mean 8, die 2 mean 5.33, die 3 known at 9). Informed traders (35%) trade whenever my quote is wrong by the settlement value. Noise traders (65%) trade around the fair value with sd 5.7.

Maximising expected profit over the grid gives a bid near 17.0 and an ask near 27.9. The ask is flat between 27.8 and 28.0, and 27.9 is marginally best. The wide spread reflects the informed-trader cost on the 0.1 grid. Position -1 doesn't change expected profit, so I'm not skewing for inventory.

{"bid": 17.0, "ask": 27.9}

Fair value is 19 + E[die 2] = 19 + 32/6 ≈ 24.33, since die 2 is hidden and uniform over {0, 2, 4, 8, 8, 10}. The possible settlement values are 19, 21, 23, 27, 27, 29.

Each side's expected profit is the noise-trader term plus the informed-trader term. The informed traders only trade against a quote that is wrong for the value they see, so the best quotes sit where that loss is small. On the ask, the expected profit peaks near 29.0, where no informed buyer can exist. On the bid, it peaks near 19.0, where no informed seller can exist. Trades at those prices are pure noise trades, so they reveal nothing that would move the fair value for quote 6.

{"bid": 19.0, "ask": 29.0}

Fair value is about 14: E[die 1] = 6 and E[die 2] = 8. No trade at quote 1 slightly downweights extreme sums, but the posterior mean stays near 14.

Balancing noise-trader spread capture against informed-trader adverse selection, the expected-profit-maximising bid is about 9.8 to 10 and the ask about 18.2 to 18.4. I'll quote 9.8 and 18.3.

{"bid": 9.8, "ask": 18.3}

Card 1 is 9, so the remaining 51 cards sum to 355 and each unseen card averages 355/51 ≈ 6.96. The public fair value is 9 + 2 × 6.96 ≈ 22.92. The sum of the two unseen cards has a standard deviation of about 5.2.

With zero inventory, I set the ask where the expected profit on a sale is roughly zero after adverse selection from the informed trader. That gives an ask about 2.9 above fair value, near 25.8. The bid is symmetric, near 20.0.

{"bid": 20.0, "ask": 25.8}

The public fair value is the expected sum of the remaining two cards, which is about 9 + 2 × 6.96 ≈ 22.9. Card 2 is still hidden, so the fair value is not yet updated for it.

Against that centre, I estimate the spread that breaks even on adverse selection (about 0 at a bid of 20.0 and slightly positive at an ask of 26.0). With a short position of -1, I'll lean slightly toward buying, so I keep the bid at the breakeven level rather than moving it lower.

{"bid": 20.0, "ask": 26.0}

Quote 6 is the last quote, so only this trade's expected edge matters. Cards 1 and 2 are 9 and 5, and card 3 is uniform over the 50 unseen cards (mean 7), so the public fair value is 9 + 5 + 7 = 21. Settlement S = 14 + card 3 ranges over 15 to 27, and its distribution is symmetric about 21. Noise valuations are Normal(21, 6.4).

Working through the edge on each side (informed traders trade only when S is outside my quote, noise traders trade on their valuation), the optimal ask sits at 27 and the optimal bid at 15. At 27, P(S>27) = 0, so an informed buyer can never hit the ask, and the noise-trader term is still favourable. Any higher ask loses noise flow for little gain. The bid is the mirror image: 15 is the lowest settlement value, so an informed seller can never hit it. Neither the trade history nor the absence of trades at quote 5 carries information about card 3, since every informed trade would have been outside [15, 27] anyway. So the quote is unchanged from quote 5.

{"bid": 15, "ask": 27}

Card 1 is 4, so the public fair value is 4 + 2 × (360/51) ≈ 18.1. Card 2 is not revealed until before stage 3, so it stays at 18.1 for this quote. The sum has a standard deviation of about 5.2, and noise valuations have a standard deviation of 6.4.

For each side, I maximised expected profit against the mixture of informed and noise counterparties. Against the ask, the best level is around 25 (expected edge about 0.37). Against the bid, the best level is around 11.5 (also about 0.37). Being long one unit doesn't change the expected-value calculation, since the position marks to fair value, so I didn't skew the quotes for inventory.

{"bid": 11.5, "ask": 25.0}

Fair value is 4 + 7 + E[card 3] = 11 + 7.06 ≈ 18.06. Card 3 is uniform over the 50 remaining cards, so the settlement S = 11 + X, with S ranging from 12 to 24.

